1
00:00:01,300 --> 00:00:05,363
Sixty-three years ago, a burst of radio
waves left a distant world,

2
00:00:05,403 --> 00:00:07,077
and began crossing the dark.

3
00:00:07,820 --> 00:00:11,016
It travelled for six decades, while humans
walked on the Moon,

4
00:00:11,056 --> 00:00:12,216
and built the internet.

5
00:00:12,959 --> 00:00:15,799
Between twenty twenty-five and twenty
twenty-six,

6
00:00:15,839 --> 00:00:18,503
a telescope in South Africa finally caught
it.

7
00:00:19,246 --> 00:00:22,073
The headlines screamed: a signal from
another world.

8
00:00:22,113 --> 00:00:24,444
Is somebody out there trying to talk to us?

9
00:00:25,187 --> 00:00:29,659
The short answer is no. The real answer is
stranger, and far more exciting.

10
00:00:30,401 --> 00:00:31,305
Let's dive in.

11
00:00:33,711 --> 00:00:37,914
In September twenty twenty-six, a team led
by Kevin Ortiz Ceballos,

12
00:00:37,954 --> 00:00:42,853
at the Harvard and Smithsonian Center for
Astrophysics, posted a paper online.

13
00:00:43,596 --> 00:00:45,368
Their claim: for the first time,

14
00:00:45,408 --> 00:00:49,616
radio waves have been traced directly to a
planet outside our solar system.

15
00:00:50,358 --> 00:00:52,477
Not to a star. To the planet itself.

16
00:00:53,220 --> 00:00:57,413
It's called Beta Pictoris b, roughly sixty-
three light-years away.

17
00:00:57,453 --> 00:01:00,941
Some reports say sixty, others sixty-four.
Same planet.

18
00:01:01,684 --> 00:01:05,958
One caveat: this paper is a preprint. It
hasn't been peer reviewed yet.

19
00:01:08,264 --> 00:01:10,390
How far is sixty-three light-years?

20
00:01:10,430 --> 00:01:14,166
Light covers nearly ten trillion kilometers
in a single year.

21
00:01:14,206 --> 00:01:15,961
Multiply that by sixty-three.

22
00:01:16,704 --> 00:01:20,529
No spacecraft we've built could make that
trip in a human lifetime.

23
00:01:20,569 --> 00:01:23,356
Voyager One would need more than a million
years.

24
00:01:24,099 --> 00:01:28,755
Our destination: the star Beta Pictoris, in
the southern constellation Pictor,

25
00:01:28,795 --> 00:01:29,959
the Painter's Easel.

26
00:01:30,702 --> 00:01:34,291
It's young, bright, and white. About twenty-
three million years old,

27
00:01:34,331 --> 00:01:38,347
and roughly twice the mass of our Sun, which
makes it practically a newborn.

28
00:01:39,090 --> 00:01:42,003
Around it swirls an edge-on disk of dust and
debris,

29
00:01:42,043 --> 00:01:44,217
the leftover rubble of planet-building.

30
00:01:46,322 --> 00:01:49,976
Inside that disk, about ten astronomical
units from its star,

31
00:01:50,016 --> 00:01:54,821
roughly where Saturn sits in our own solar
system, orbits the star of our story.

32
00:01:55,564 --> 00:01:59,781
Beta Pictoris b. A gas giant about twelve
times the mass of Jupiter,

33
00:01:59,821 --> 00:02:03,037
taking roughly twenty-four years to circle
its star.

34
00:02:03,780 --> 00:02:08,341
And it spins incredibly fast: one full
rotation in only eight to nine hours.

35
00:02:09,084 --> 00:02:12,789
A heavyweight planet with a blistering spin,
that's the secret.

36
00:02:12,829 --> 00:02:17,782
A fast-spinning world with a powerful
magnetic field is a natural radio
transmitter.

37
00:02:18,525 --> 00:02:20,901
Better still, its star is magnetically
quiet,

38
00:02:20,941 --> 00:02:23,317
so it doesn't drown out the planet's
whisper.

39
00:02:25,623 --> 00:02:27,878
To hear it, the team used MeerKAT,

40
00:02:27,918 --> 00:02:31,254
a radio telescope in South Africa's Northern
Cape,

41
00:02:31,294 --> 00:02:34,899
about ninety kilometers outside the town of
Carnarvon.

42
00:02:35,642 --> 00:02:40,480
It's sixty-four dishes, spread across the
desert, working together as one giant ear.

43
00:02:41,223 --> 00:02:43,422
That trick is called interferometry.

44
00:02:43,462 --> 00:02:48,459
Combining every dish lets astronomers
pinpoint where in the sky a radio wave came

45
00:02:48,499 --> 00:02:48,770
from.

46
00:02:49,512 --> 00:02:53,593
It's a stepping stone to the Square
Kilometre Array, a future mega-telescope.

47
00:02:54,336 --> 00:02:56,874
They pointed it at Beta Pictoris four times,

48
00:02:56,914 --> 00:03:01,678
across twenty twenty-five and twenty twenty-
six. Every time, they heard something.

49
00:03:02,421 --> 00:03:06,072
Short bursts of radio emission, that
brightened and faded in moments.

50
00:03:08,378 --> 00:03:11,117
But hearing a signal isn't enough. On the
sky,

51
00:03:11,157 --> 00:03:14,501
the planet sits at most half an arcsecond
from its star,

52
00:03:14,541 --> 00:03:17,643
the width of a coin seen from seven
kilometers away.

53
00:03:18,386 --> 00:03:21,735
Earlier searches found radio signals from
planetary systems,

54
00:03:21,775 --> 00:03:25,407
but couldn't prove whether the planet or its
star was the source.

55
00:03:26,150 --> 00:03:29,877
So the team used cosmic lighthouses: nine
distant quasars,

56
00:03:29,917 --> 00:03:32,929
precisely measured by the Gaia space
telescope,

57
00:03:32,969 --> 00:03:37,151
plus one carefully located calibrator, as
fixed reference points.

58
00:03:37,894 --> 00:03:42,754
Anchoring the radio image to those points
locked it onto the sky with astonishing

59
00:03:42,794 --> 00:03:43,298
accuracy.

60
00:03:44,041 --> 00:03:48,872
The result: the signal lined up with planet
b, and was far enough from the star,

61
00:03:48,912 --> 00:03:52,160
and from the planet Beta Pictoris c, to rule
both out.

62
00:03:54,466 --> 00:03:57,019
So what is this signal? Think of the
northern lights.

63
00:03:57,762 --> 00:04:02,459
On Earth, charged particles are funneled by
our magnetic field toward the poles,

64
00:04:02,499 --> 00:04:06,782
slam into the upper atmosphere, and glow.
They also give off radio waves,

65
00:04:06,822 --> 00:04:07,907
which we can't see.

66
00:04:08,650 --> 00:04:10,747
Jupiter does it far more powerfully.

67
00:04:10,787 --> 00:04:15,555
Its radio auroras are strong enough for
amateur radio astronomers to pick up from

68
00:04:15,595 --> 00:04:15,911
Earth.

69
00:04:16,654 --> 00:04:20,118
Beta Pictoris b appears to do it on a whole
new scale.

70
00:04:20,158 --> 00:04:25,243
The physics has a mouthful of a name: the
electron cyclotron maser instability.

71
00:04:25,986 --> 00:04:31,343
Electrons spiral along magnetic field lines,
and emit intense, beamed radio waves.

72
00:04:33,649 --> 00:04:34,431
Here's the key.

73
00:04:34,471 --> 00:04:38,651
The highest frequency of that emission
depends directly on the magnetic field

74
00:04:38,691 --> 00:04:40,240
strength where it's produced.

75
00:04:40,983 --> 00:04:44,000
The bursts reached about three and a half
gigahertz,

76
00:04:44,040 --> 00:04:46,351
the very top of what MeerKAT can detect.

77
00:04:47,094 --> 00:04:51,236
So the planet's magnetic field must be at
least twelve hundred and fifty gauss.

78
00:04:51,979 --> 00:04:57,071
Earth's surface field is about half a gauss.
Jupiter's is between four and fourteen.

79
00:04:57,111 --> 00:05:00,614
Beta Pictoris b is thousands of times
stronger than Earth.

80
00:05:01,357 --> 00:05:05,469
And that's a lower limit. The planet may
emit at even higher frequencies,

81
00:05:05,509 --> 00:05:07,516
beyond what the telescope could see.

82
00:05:08,259 --> 00:05:12,288
It's the first direct measurement of an
exoplanet's magnetic field.

83
00:05:12,328 --> 00:05:15,749
Astronomer Yvette Cendes called it, quote,
an incredibly,

84
00:05:15,789 --> 00:05:20,729
incredibly strong magnetic field, much
stronger than anything in our solar system.

85
00:05:23,035 --> 00:05:26,339
What powers those bursts? The team tested
several ideas.

86
00:05:27,082 --> 00:05:31,778
The star's wind hitting the planet? That
falls short by roughly a thousand times.

87
00:05:32,521 --> 00:05:37,193
A moon, like Io feeding Jupiter's auroras?
Still more than ten times too weak.

88
00:05:37,936 --> 00:05:40,172
The leading idea: that blistering spin,

89
00:05:40,212 --> 00:05:44,725
driving electrical currents between the
planet's magnetic bubble and its upper

90
00:05:44,765 --> 00:05:45,367
atmosphere.

91
00:05:46,110 --> 00:05:47,980
And a hint: in one observation,

92
00:05:48,020 --> 00:05:51,615
the first and third bursts arrived about
eight hours apart,

93
00:05:51,655 --> 00:05:56,668
close to the planet's rotation period. Like
a lighthouse sweeping past us. A hint,

94
00:05:56,708 --> 00:05:57,284
not proof.

95
00:05:59,590 --> 00:06:02,540
So why are astronomers confident this isn't
a message?

96
00:06:03,283 --> 00:06:06,625
First, it's broadband, spread across many
frequencies.

97
00:06:06,665 --> 00:06:11,008
Searches for alien technology usually hunt
for the opposite: a narrow,

98
00:06:11,048 --> 00:06:13,576
pure tone that's hard for nature to make.

99
00:06:14,319 --> 00:06:18,552
Second, it's circularly polarized: the waves
corkscrew through space,

100
00:06:18,592 --> 00:06:23,072
a fingerprint of auroral radio emission from
a strongly magnetized world.

101
00:06:23,815 --> 00:06:28,242
Third, the magnetic field it implies matches
what theory predicts for young,

102
00:06:28,282 --> 00:06:29,535
massive giant planets.

103
00:06:30,278 --> 00:06:34,932
Harvard physicist Avi Loeb, famous for
taking alien possibilities seriously,

104
00:06:34,972 --> 00:06:39,070
says this one is most likely natural, like
Earth's northern lights.

105
00:06:39,813 --> 00:06:41,612
That doesn't make it less amazing.

106
00:06:41,652 --> 00:06:45,506
A planet is shouting across sixty-three
light-years, and we can hear it.

107
00:06:47,812 --> 00:06:49,025
Why does this matter?

108
00:06:49,065 --> 00:06:53,976
A planet's magnetic field shapes how its
atmosphere interacts with its star's wind,

109
00:06:54,016 --> 00:06:57,137
and carries clues about its deep interior.
Until now,

110
00:06:57,177 --> 00:07:00,955
we had no direct way to measure those fields
around other stars.

111
00:07:01,698 --> 00:07:03,579
Earlier searches came up empty:

112
00:07:03,619 --> 00:07:07,234
a twenty twenty-two pilot of eight directly
imaged planets,

113
00:07:07,274 --> 00:07:12,190
and a twenty twenty-four search of Beta
Pictoris b itself, at lower frequencies.

114
00:07:12,932 --> 00:07:16,545
This time it may have worked because this
planet is a magnetic heavyweight.

115
00:07:17,288 --> 00:07:21,861
The team has already identified seven more
directly imaged giant planets,

116
00:07:21,901 --> 00:07:25,589
in five nearby systems, that could be
studied the same way,

117
00:07:25,629 --> 00:07:28,812
with telescopes five to seven times more
sensitive.

118
00:07:29,555 --> 00:07:31,132
Still, this is one preprint.

119
00:07:31,172 --> 00:07:34,770
Peer review and follow-up observations will
put it to the test.

120
00:07:37,076 --> 00:07:38,793
And here's a humbling thought.

121
00:07:38,833 --> 00:07:43,360
Our own radio and television broadcasts from
the nineteen sixties are only now

122
00:07:43,400 --> 00:07:47,225
arriving in that neighborhood, far too faint
for anyone to notice.

123
00:07:47,968 --> 00:07:51,794
Do you think we'll ever pick up a real
message? Tell me in the comments.

124
00:07:51,834 --> 00:07:55,122
And if this made the universe feel a little
bigger, subscribe,

125
00:07:55,162 --> 00:07:56,894
and I'll see you in the next one.
